WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.380
Part 2. You will hear an archaeologist, who is part of a community project to excavate

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:13.620
an ancient village, talking to members of the public who are visiting the site. First,

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:17.180
you have some time to look at questions 11 to 16.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:22.519
Hello, I'm Hayden. I'm one of the archaeologists investigating the site here at Bidcaster.

00:01:22.519 --> 00:01:27.720
This is the third summer for this community project, and most of the people digging here

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:33.959
are volunteers. I'm a full-time archaeologist for the town council, but I was asked to join

00:01:33.959 --> 00:01:41.320
the project by NHA, a charity which sets up projects like this up and down the country.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:46.599
As you can see, we're next to Bidcaster Castle, which is great, because the owners let us

00:01:46.599 --> 00:01:54.680
use their facilities. So, how did we get to where we are today? Many archaeology projects

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:01.080
happen when an ancient object is found, and in our case, that object was a gold coin.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:07.720
Coins are often found by people using metal detectors to look for things buried in the ground,

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:15.880
or coins are uncovered when wild animals like rabbits have been digging tunnels. Here,

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:21.479
a walker found it on the ground after a rainstorm washed away some of the earth and sand.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:28.360
When the story of the gold coin hit the news, Peter Swift, an amateur historian,

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:33.080
contacted me to say he believed there had been a village on this site,

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:40.039
centuries before the castle was built. Just by chance, the team found some old maps and

00:02:40.039 --> 00:02:46.600
documents in our library, which showed 500-year-old drawings of ruined buildings

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:52.199
on the grassy area between the outer stone walls of the castle and the river.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:59.479
We knew then we were on to something. Over the three summers the team has been here,

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:05.000
we've found the remains of several buildings, and more broken pots than you can count.

00:03:06.039 --> 00:03:11.479
Normally, you'd expect to find brooches and other jewellery, but we're still waiting to

00:03:11.479 --> 00:03:18.039
uncover any such items. The people who once lived here were skilled at making tools from

00:03:18.039 --> 00:03:21.559
animal bones, as you'll see when you visit the exhibition.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:28.759
Besides the discovery of the village, we've also found evidence of human activity on the

00:03:28.759 --> 00:03:35.559
other side of the river. No other houses or huts so far, but we can see the borders of an

00:03:35.559 --> 00:03:42.440
ancient field system. At one point we found a long wall and thought it was an ancient palace,

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:49.000
but it turned out to be a modern wall. This summer's work will end soon, but we'll

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:55.639
be back next summer. In the meantime, we're putting on a series of guided tours for school

00:03:55.639 --> 00:04:01.960
groups this autumn. Oh, and maybe you saw the TV documentary about our project?

00:04:02.759 --> 00:04:06.919
That suggested the objects we've found are going to the town's museum,

00:04:07.479 --> 00:04:09.720
but we don't know that for sure yet.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:19.799
Before you hear the rest of the talk, you have some time to look at questions 17 to 20.

00:04:31.959 --> 00:04:53.799
Now listen and answer questions 17 to 20.

00:04:54.279 --> 00:05:00.760
When you enter the site, please make sure you keep to the paths at all times. There are a few

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:07.160
other things, the highlights of the site if you like, that I want to mention. Take a look at the

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:16.359
map. Our present location is marked at the bottom. This year we've identified the foundations of an

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:26.040
ancient bridge, and it's really exciting today because a team of divers are in the river searching

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:33.239
for lost objects. To reach the bridge, take the main path ahead of you, go straight on,

00:05:33.799 --> 00:05:40.839
and keep going till the path bends to the left. You'll see a smaller track leading off to the

00:05:40.839 --> 00:05:45.079
right. Follow that to take you to the river, where the divers are.

00:05:46.839 --> 00:05:52.600
You might be interested to see the rubbish pit. This is very near the castle walls,

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:58.519
in the north-west corner of the site. It actually dates to the time of the castle,

00:05:58.519 --> 00:06:04.119
and not the ancient village. We found oyster shells and fish bones,

00:06:04.119 --> 00:06:07.640
and we assume they were thrown from the castle kitchen above.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:15.160
One area we excavated in the first summer uncovered the site of a meeting hall. We knew

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:21.880
it was an important building because it had two rows of postholes, deep enough to support a large

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:29.079
roof. It's the largest structure in the central area of the site, next to the current excavation

00:06:29.079 --> 00:06:36.679
area. Last year we discovered a fish pond in the ancient village. Normally these were beside

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:44.119
a river. The pond here is further away, but it's possible the river has moved slightly.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:51.480
Anyway, to get there from here, you turn right at the first information board you come to,

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:57.399
and follow the path into the trees. Before you come out of the trees,

00:06:57.399 --> 00:07:02.679
you'll see it on your right. If you reach the river, you've gone too far.

00:07:02.679 --> 00:07:05.640
So, does anyone have any questions?

00:07:32.679 --> 00:07:33.720
you

